<h2 id="why-houston">Why Houston is one of the best cities for side hustles</h2>
<p>A few things make side gigs in Houston unusually productive:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Sprawl means longer trips and more demand.</strong> The metro stretches from Katy to Baytown and from The Woodlands to Pearland. Long distances push up per-trip pay for drivers and couriers, and busy commuters happily pay someone else to run errands, clean cars and handle chores.</li>
  <li><strong>Huge, stable employers.</strong> The Texas Medical Center is the largest medical complex in the world, the Energy Corridor along I-10 houses major oil and gas offices, the Port of Houston keeps logistics humming, and NASA's Johnson Space Center anchors Clear Lake. That's a lot of well-paid professionals who outsource their weekends.</li>
  <li><strong>Two airports.</strong> George Bush Intercontinental (IAH) and Hobby (HOU) generate steady rideshare demand from early morning to late night.</li>
  <li><strong>Heat and storms create work.</strong> Humidity grows mildew on every driveway, pools need care most of the year, and hurricane season (June through November) brings prep, cleanup and generator help.</li>
  <li><strong>A big event calendar.</strong> The Houston Livestock Show and Rodeo fills NRG Park for about three weeks every late winter, the Offshore Technology Conference packs hotels each spring, and the Astros, Texans, Rockets and Dynamo keep game-day crowds coming.</li>
</ul>
<p>Houston is also one of the most diverse big cities in the US, so a second language has real market value here.</p>

