<h2 id="driving">Driving and delivery: Houston's fastest cash</h2>
<p>If you are wondering how to make money in Houston this month, app work is the quickest route. The difference between a good and a bad week in Houston is almost entirely where and when you drive.</p>
<div class="hustle-grid">
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="rideshare">1. Rideshare driving</h3>
    <p>Houston's distances make rides longer and fares bigger. The money windows are pre-dawn airport runs to IAH and Hobby, Friday and Saturday nights in Midtown, Washington Avenue and Montrose, shift changes around the Medical Center, and post-game rushes at Daikin Park, Toyota Center and NRG Stadium. Our <a href="/lyft-uber/">Lyft and Uber guide</a> covers sign-up basics.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $15–$25/hr before gas and wear</li><li><b>Startup:</b> Eligible car, insurance</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Early birds, night owls</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="delivery">2. Food and grocery delivery</h3>
    <p>DoorDash, Uber Eats, Instacart and Walmart Spark are busy all over town. Suburbs like Katy, Cypress, Sugar Land and The Woodlands tend to tip better and have easy parking, while the Galleria area and Rice Village pack restaurants close together. Rainy evenings and brutal August afternoons, when nobody wants to leave the AC, often pay the most.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $15–$22/hr before gas</li><li><b>Startup:</b> Car, bike or e-bike</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Flexible evenings</li></ul>
  </div>
</div>
