<h2 id="heat-services">Heat, humidity and home services (best pay per hour)</h2>
<p>Houston's climate is hard on houses, cars and yards. Homeowners in the suburbs and the older neighborhoods inside the Loop will pay well for someone dependable to keep up.</p>
<div class="hustle-grid">
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="pressure-washing">3. Pressure washing</h3>
    <p>Humidity turns driveways, fences and north-facing siding green. That makes pressure washing one of the most reliable side jobs in Houston, with demand nearly year-round. Single jobs often pay $150 to $400, and before-and-after photos sell themselves in Nextdoor and neighborhood Facebook groups across Pearland, Friendswood and Kingwood. An <a href="/side-hustle-gear/">electric pressure washer</a> handles most residential work.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $150–$400 per job</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $250–$600</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Weekend warriors</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="mobile-detailing">4. Mobile car detailing</h3>
    <p>Houston is a truck-and-SUV town, and nobody wants to sit in a car wash line in July. Bring the detail to them: office parking lots in the Energy Corridor and Greenway Plaza, or driveways in Cinco Ranch and Sienna. Interior details are an easy upsell after Galveston beach trips.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $100–$250 per full detail</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $200–$600</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Detail-obsessed hustlers</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="pool-cleaning">5. Pool cleaning</h3>
    <p>Backyard pools are everywhere in the suburbs, and Houston's long swim season means they need weekly care for most of the year. Build a tight route of 10 to 15 pools in one subdivision and the income becomes predictable. Learn water chemistry before you take on clients, and note that Texas generally treats pool service as a taxable service.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $25–$45 per weekly visit</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $150–$400 in tools and test kits</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Route builders</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="rental-cleaning">6. Short-term rental turnover cleaning</h3>
    <p>Houston now requires short-term rentals inside city limits to register with the city, which has pushed hosts toward professional, reliable operations. Turnovers near the Medical Center, NRG Park and Downtown are busiest during Rodeo season, big conventions and hospital-visit stays. One good property manager can fill your week.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $25–$45/hr</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $75–$150 in supplies</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Detail-oriented people</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="interpreting">7. Bilingual interpreting and translation</h3>
    <p>Hospitals, clinics, law offices and schools across Houston need interpreters every day, especially for Spanish and Vietnamese. Start with document translation and community interpreting, then consider medical interpreter training if you want the higher-paying hospital work.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $25–$60/hr</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $0–$300 for training</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Fluent bilingual speakers</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="handyman">8. Handyman and storm prep</h3>
    <p>Storms like Hurricane Beryl in 2024 left much of the metro without power for days, and homeowners remember. Offer hurricane shutter installs, gutter cleaning, fence repair, ceiling fan swaps and TV mounting, plus debris cleanup after storms. Stay with small jobs and refer out anything that needs a licensed electrician or plumber.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $40–$75/hr</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $100–$400 in tools</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Handy people with a truck</li></ul>
  </div>
</div>

