<h2 id="get-started">How to start a side hustle in Houston this month</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Pick one hustle that fits your hours.</strong> Choose based on the time you can protect every week, not the time you wish you had.</li>
  <li><strong>Choose one part of town.</strong> Houston is too big to serve everywhere. Pick your own side of the metro to cut drive time and build word of mouth.</li>
  <li><strong>Set an earnings target.</strong> Work backward from a monthly number to hours per week with the <a href="/tools/income-calculator/">income calculator</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Get listed where people already look.</strong> App sign-ups for gig work; Nextdoor, neighborhood Facebook groups and a Google Business Profile for local services.</li>
  <li><strong>Land three clients and ask for reviews.</strong> Early reviews beat any ad.</li>
  <li><strong>Mark the calendar.</strong> Add Rodeo dates, OTC week, Astros and Texans home games and hurricane season so you can plan surges and slow stretches.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="texas-taxes">Texas taxes and Houston rules for side income</h2>
<p>Texas is one of the simplest states for side income, but there are a few traps:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>No state income tax.</strong> You owe federal income tax and 15.3% self-employment tax on net profit, and that's it. Set aside roughly 20 to 25% and pay quarterly once you expect to owe $1,000 or more.</li>
  <li><strong>Sales tax applies to more than products.</strong> The state rate is 6.25%, and Houston's combined rate is typically 8.25%. Texas also taxes some services, including many lawn, landscaping, pool and cleaning jobs, with exceptions. Get a free sales tax permit from the Comptroller if your work is taxable.</li>
  <li><strong>Assumed name certificate.</strong> Operating under a brand as a sole proprietor means filing a DBA with the Harris County Clerk (or your county).</li>
  <li><strong>Short-term rentals.</strong> Since January 1, 2026, every short-term rental inside Houston city limits needs a city Certificate of Registration, with a $275 annual fee, plus state and local hotel occupancy taxes.</li>
  <li><strong>Rideshare.</strong> Texas regulates Uber and Lyft at the state level, so you don't need a separate Houston chauffeur license to drive for the apps.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/side-job-hustles-in-texas-emerging-trends-and-opportunities-for-all/">Texas side hustle guide</a> covers statewide trends in more depth.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>Quick tip:</strong> Expect a 1099-K or 1099-NEC from apps and clients. Track mileage from the moment you open the app; in a city this spread out, the mileage deduction can erase much of your federal tax on driving profit.</div>

